+//
+// One value per outcome a person can be told apart. NTB-11 requires the
+// refusal to say why, and a single `bool` cannot: "nothing happened" is the
+// answer this feature already gives too often -- §5 of
+// `docs/NEWTAB_BACKGROUND_CONTRACT.md` records that a rejected asset and an
+// absent one look identical on screen -- and the picker is the one place in
+// the feature that holds the bytes, the rule and the person at the same
+// moment.
+enum class InstallResult {
+  kInstalled,
+  // The file could not be measured or read at all. A file deleted between the
+  // dialog closing and this running lands here, which is the honest answer.
+  kUnreadable,
+  kTooLarge,
+  // The first bytes are not PNG, JPEG or WebP.
+  //
+  // **The refusal cannot name what the file actually is, and that is a rule
+  // rather than an omission.** Saying "this is a GIF" would mean declaring a
+  // GIF signature, and NTB-2 forbids this build from declaring any excluded
+  // format's signature -- the guard exists because a table of refused
+  // signatures is one edit away from being a table of accepted ones. So the
+  // sentence a person gets names the three formats that are allowed and the
+  // fact that these bytes are none of them.
+  kUnsupportedFormat,
+  // Validated, and then the copy failed. Nothing was removed.
+  kWriteFailed,
+};
+
+// The outcome, and the measurements behind it.
+//
+// `size` is the size of the file the person chose, in bytes, or -1 when it
+// could not be measured. It is carried even on success because the caller
+// composing the sentence should not have to measure the file a second time to
+// say "112 MB, and the limit is 100 MB" -- both numbers come from the browser,
+// which is the only party that has read either.
+struct InstallOutcome {
+  InstallResult result = InstallResult::kUnreadable;
+  int64_t size = -1;
+};
+
+// Copy `source` into this profile's background directory, if the rules admit
+// it.
+//
+// Blocking: measures, reads and copies, so it must run somewhere that may
+// block.
+//
+// **Byte for byte, never decoded and re-emitted -- NTB-11.** `base::CopyFile`
+// and nothing else. A picker that decoded an image and wrote it back out would
+// silently turn an APNG into a photograph, which is the whole animated half of
+// this feature disappearing without anyone being told. Upstream's own
+// *Upload from device* does the same thing for the same reason
+// (`CopyFileToProfilePath` in
+// `chrome/browser/ui/webui/side_panel/customize_chrome/`), so this is not a
+// simplification of what a real picker does.
+//
+// **Validated before it lands, and nothing is written on refusal.** The size
+// and the first bytes are read from `source`; a refusal returns before
+// anything is created, removed or overwritten.
+//
+// **The validation here is for the sentence, not for safety, and knowing which
+// matters.** `ReadInstalledBackground` validates every file it serves, every
+// time it serves it, so a file that changed on disk after this returned would
+// still never be served -- it would simply go back to looking like no
+// background at all. What this pass buys is the ability to say *why* at the
+// moment a person can still do something about it.
+//
+// **The destination name comes from the bytes, not from the source's
+// name.** A file picked as `holiday.jpeg` is written as `newtab-background.jpg`
+// because `DetectFormat` said JPEG. §3's rule -- one name per permitted format
+// -- exists so that no file in this feature is named something untrue, and
+// taking the extension from the source would break it on the first file whose
+// name disagreed with its contents.
+//
+// **The other names are removed after the copy succeeds.** A profile holds at
+// most one background: `AssetFileNames()` is searched in order, so a leftover
+// `.png` would keep winning over a newly chosen `.webp` and the picker would
+// appear to do nothing. Removal happens after rather than before, so a failed
+// copy leaves the profile exactly as it was.
+InstallOutcome InstallBackground(const base::FilePath& source,
+                                 const base::FilePath& profile_path);
+
+// Remove this profile's background, whatever its format. Blocking.
+//
+// Returns whether anything was removed. Afterwards the profile falls through
+// to the install directory again, which is the behaviour §3 kept deliberately:
+// a machine-wide image is what a profile sees until it chooses its own, and
+// again once it gives that up.
+bool RemoveBackground(const base::FilePath& profile_path);
+
+// Whether this profile has a background of its own, as opposed to seeing the
+// install directory's. Blocking.
+//
+// Distinct from `AvailableFromCache`, which answers whether *a* background will
+// be shown from either directory. The settings surface needs the narrower
+// question, because "Remove" can only act on the profile's own.
+bool HasProfileBackground(const base::FilePath& profile_path);
+
 }  // namespace sunshine::background
 
 #endif  // CHROME_BROWSER_UI_SUNSHINE_NEWTAB_BACKGROUND_H_
